1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,050
You wake up on the deck of a boat. It's July,

2
00:00:03,050 --> 00:00:06,250
the air is already warm, and the river has swollen so wide

3
00:00:06,250 --> 00:00:09,250
you can barely see the far bank.

4
00:00:09,250 --> 00:00:12,460
Forty men are sleeping around you, wrapped in linen.

5
00:00:12,460 --> 00:00:15,060
Behind you rises a pale cliff of limestone

6
00:00:15,060 --> 00:00:17,800
with fresh chisel marks all over it.

7
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:20,820
And a few feet away, a man is already awake.

8
00:00:20,820 --> 00:00:23,710
He's holding a reed pen and a sheet of papyrus.

9
00:00:23,710 --> 00:00:25,320
He writes one line:

10
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:27,200
where the team spent the night.

11
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:29,070
Then he puts it away.

12
00:00:29,070 --> 00:00:30,670
His name is Merer.

13
00:00:30,670 --> 00:00:31,870
He's your boss.

14
00:00:31,870 --> 00:00:34,340
And today, like most days this summer,

15
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:38,340
your job is to move a block of stone about as heavy as a pickup truck

16
00:00:38,340 --> 00:00:42,280
across this river to a building site 10 miles away.

17
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:44,060
Here's the strange part.

18
00:00:44,060 --> 00:00:46,550
That sheet of papyrus survived.

19
00:00:46,550 --> 00:00:48,950
Four and a half thousand years later,

20
00:00:48,950 --> 00:00:52,320
archaeologists pulled it out of a cave on the Red Sea coast.

21
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:55,640
It's the oldest inscribed papyrus ever found.

22
00:00:55,640 --> 00:00:58,930
And it was written by a man who helped build the Great Pyramid.

23
00:00:58,930 --> 00:01:00,520
So forget the whips.

24
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:02,170
Forget the aliens.

25
00:01:02,170 --> 00:01:06,170
For the first time, we can ask the people who were actually there:

26
00:01:06,170 --> 00:01:08,610
what did the pyramid builders do all day?

27
00:01:08,610 --> 00:01:09,610
Who fed them?

28
00:01:09,610 --> 00:01:10,920
Who patched them up?

29
00:01:10,920 --> 00:01:13,230
And why did they do it at all?

30
00:01:13,230 --> 00:01:15,770
The answer starts with that one line.

31
00:01:15,770 --> 00:01:17,120
Write it down.

32
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:21,410
To understand Merer's day, we first need to talk about his king.

33
00:01:21,410 --> 00:01:26,080
Khufu rules Egypt around 2,600 years before Jesus.

34
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:28,240
Nobody agrees on his exact dates;

35
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:34,500
one common estimate puts his reign at roughly 2589 to 2566 BC.

36
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:37,240
To his people, he isn't just a ruler.

37
00:01:37,240 --> 00:01:39,250
He's a god living on Earth,

38
00:01:39,250 --> 00:01:42,990
and when he dies, he has to make it safely to the sky.

39
00:01:42,990 --> 00:01:45,130
That trip needs a launch pad.

40
00:01:45,130 --> 00:01:49,410
So Khufu orders the biggest building the world has ever seen:

41
00:01:49,410 --> 00:01:52,010
a pyramid about 481 feet tall,

42
00:01:52,010 --> 00:01:56,160
made of something like 2.3 million blocks.

43
00:01:56,160 --> 00:02:01,670
It will stay the tallest human-made structure on the planet for about 3,800 years.

44
00:02:01,670 --> 00:02:03,260
To put that in time:

45
00:02:03,260 --> 00:02:07,920
Cleopatra lived closer to the Moon landing than to the building of this pyramid.

46
00:02:07,920 --> 00:02:09,750
Now, I know what you're thinking.

47
00:02:09,750 --> 00:02:12,030
Somebody had to do the actual work.

48
00:02:12,030 --> 00:02:14,630
And for about 2,500 years, the world's answer

49
00:02:14,630 --> 00:02:17,710
came from one Greek tourist.
